An empirical mathematical relation can be assessed by RSM via regression technique with predicted responses and inflicting variables. Quadratic equations were recognized, which defines a relationship between the factors and their responses. In Eqs. (7) and (8), A, B, and C are ascribed to $\mathbf{wt\%}$ of PEG, ${\bf w t\%}$ of chitosan, and the thickness of the membrane, respectively. The interactions between the factors and their responses have antagonistic influences, as illustrated by negative model terms in Eqs. (7) and (8).
## 3.5. Interactions of variables
Figs. 5 and 6 illustrate the 3D charts of the binary interactions of the variables on MG rejection and flux. The interaction among PEG $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ , chitosan $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ , and flux are revealed in Fig. 5(a).
By raising PEG from 2 to 10 $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ , the flux increases by $58\left(\mathrm{L},\mathrm{m}^{-2}\right.$ . $\mathbf{h}^{-1})$ ), because PEG plays the pivotal role of pore generator.
Fig. 5(b) shows the interaction between the amount of PEG $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ and the thickness of the CPZP membrane on the flux. Flux decreases intensely from 69 to $11(\mathrm{L}.\mathrm{m}^{-2}.\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ by increasing the thickness of the membrane from 200 to $400~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Membrane thickness is a significant parameter in nanofiltration systems. By increasing the thickness of the CPZP membrane, the mass transfer resistance of the membrane becomes greater.
Fig. 5(c) displays the interaction between the thickness of the CPZP membrane and the amount of chitosan $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ on the flux. Increasing chitosan from 1 to 3 $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ decreases flux from 20 to 15. This can be attributed to the denser structure of the top layer as a result of the chitosan percentage increment, as observed in other works (Salehi et al., 2016; Salehi, Madaeni, Rajabi et al., 2012).
In light of the findings, the interaction between the thickness and chitosan amount is feeble. CPZP membrane with a thinner thickness showed better water flux due to decreased mass transfer resistance (Wu, Wang, & Field, 2014).
The interaction among the amount of PEG $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ , the amount of chitosan $\left(\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ , and the rejection of MG solution using the CPZP membrane is revealed in Fig. 6(a). Increasing chitosan from 1 to 3 (wt $\%$ increases the rejection of MG from 71 to $80~\%$ , which can be attributed to the raise of the hydroxyle and amino functional groups that act as reactive sites for adsorption of MG. Increasing the compactness of the top layer by increasing the CS amount can be another reason for the enhancement of the dye rejection. Also, raising the amount of PEG (wt $\%$ slightly affected the rejection of MG using CPZP membrane.
Fig. 6(b) displays the interaction between the thickness of the CPZP membrane and the amount of PEG $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ on the rejection of MG molecules using the CPZP membrane. In light of the outcomes, the interaction between the membrane thickness and the PEG percent weight is robust and inverse considering the rejection of MG.
The optimal conditions were obtained as; PEG of $10~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , chitosan equals $3\mathrm{\wt{\%}}$ , and the membrane thickness of $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , which simultaneously introduces the highest model-predicted flux and rejection as $79.98(\mathrm{L}.\mathrm{~m}^{-2}.\mathrm{~h}^{-1})$ and 92.91 $(\%)$ , respectively. The nanofiltration experiment was re-directed at the calculated optimal conditions for testing the validity of the model. The experimentally-measured flux of 78.94 (L $\cdot\mathrm{~m~}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ and rejection of 90.32 $(\%)$ were obtained that are close to the model-predicted results (Fig. 7). This result verified the favorability of the optimization and the calculated model.
According to Fig. 7, it was found that the rejection of MG decreased from $97.93~\%$ to $90.32\%$ over the time of $30{-}90~\mathrm{min}$ . The presence of different functional groups of CS and PVA such as −OH and $\boldsymbol{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ beside those of ZIF-8 enhanced the adsorptive interactions among MG molecules and the surface active sites. The hydrophilic nature of the CPZP membrane improved the surface hydrophilicity of the thin film composite membrane. In addition, large surface area and high porosity of ZIF-8 assist the accessibility of MG molecules to the adsorption sites and facilitate the diffusion of them into the polymer network. These properties are regarded as the main advantages of using ZIF-8 for MG removal. Mainly two types of interactions, including $\scriptstyle{\pi-\pi}$ and electrostatic one, control the adsorption mechanism. Therefore, the functional groups of CS/PVA polymer background and the extended porous network provided by the ZIF-8 nanostructures are the main reasons for the enhancement of MG rejection as well as regenerability of the CPZP membrane (Guo et al., 2016).
To summarize, assessed with other chitosan-based membranes (shown in Table 2), the primed CPZP membrane in the current study revealed competitive performance regarding lower applied pressure of 1 bar $(0.1\ \mathrm{\MPa})$ for energy-saving purpose, which results in better permeability $(78.94~\mathrm{~L}.\mathrm{m}^{-2}$ . $\ensuremath{\mathbf{h}}^{-1}$ . $\mathbf{bar}^{-1}.$ . Several reasons can be announced to describe the more reflux of the CPZP membrane in comparison to other membranes despite the less pressure. Water flux is in direct association with the porosity, the size of pores, and the membrane surface hydrophilicity. It has been stated that the transport of molecules within the membrane occurs through the inter-edge spaces between adjacent the pores within the membrane (Sharma & Purkait, 2017). In the current study, PEG (as a pore generator) was used as a hydrophilic additive inducing high porosity structures to increase the permeability and hydrophilicity of the CPZP membrane.
Also, the incorporation of ZIF-8 nanoparticles disrupts the polymer chain packing, thereby creating voids in the thin film. The created voids produce a new pathway to pass water molecules and increase water flux of the CPZP membrane.
Additionally, the functional groups on the surface of CPZP play a significant role in the water flux of CPZP membrane. Chitosan includes oxygen functional groups (−COOH and −OH), which attract water molecules that can be ascribed to strong hydrogen-bonding (CohenTanugi & Grossman, 2012). Oxygen functional groups have a high water uptake capacity that can increase the hydrophilicity of the membrane, which results in high water flux under minimum operating pressure. The water flux is inversely proportional to the thickness of the membrane for a pressure-driven process (Han, Xu, & Gao, 2013). Based on the data depicted in Table 2, CPZP membrane offers comparable performance (flux and rejection) but with the minimum trans-membrane pressure when compared with similar chitosan-modified membranes. Extremely high permeability of the CPZP membrane in comparison with similar membranes reflects its economical favorability for the filtration process.
Furthermore, the CPZP composite membrane was compared to the CPZ membrane adsorbent (CS/ PVA/ZIF-8), presented in our previous research work (Khajavian et al., 2020). The coating of CPZ thin membrane adsorbent to the surface of the PVDF support provided required mechanical stability and enabled us to apply the resultant composite membrane in dynamic nanofiltration process. This modification increases the possibility of the industrialization of the CPZ adsorptive membranes. It is also worth mentioning that our previous study was mainly focused on the optimization of the batch adsorption process variables (initial concentration, adsorbent mass, and pH), while the present study was concentrated on the optimization of CPZP membrane composition (the amount of chitosan, PEG and thickness) for application in continuous filtration of dye solution.
## 3.6. Antifouling and rejection properties
The experiments of antifouling properties were performed using deionized water and BSA aqueous solutions alternately as feed solution. Fig. 8 presented that changing feed solution from distilled water to 200 $\mathrm{mg/L}$ BSA solution causes to reduce the flux of the CPZP membrane from $78.94\mathrm{L}.\mathrm{m}^{-2}$ . $\ln^{-1}$ to $18.94\mathrm{L.m}^{-2}.\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . This is due to the fact that the BSA molecules stick to the surface of the CPZP membrane. Afterward, the CPZP membrane was cleaned from the BSA by distilled water, and the amount of flux recovery ratio (FRR) was calculated as $92.00\%$ for the CPZP membrane. The appropriate resistance of the CPZP membrane against BSA can be ascribed to the hydrophilicity of the membrane. Also, the corresponding total fouling ratio $(R_{t})$ was $76.00\%$ , with a $68.01~\%$ reversible fouling ratio $(R_{r})$ and a $7.99~\%$ irreversible fouling ratio $(R_{i r})$ . These results show that a compactly stacked foulant layer cannot be created on the membrane surface because hydraulic cleaning is able to adequately eliminate the BSA molecules with smaller particle size (Wenzel, 1936). Generally, the acceptable value of FRR during fouling test shows that the primed CPZP membrane possesses the suitable antifouling property.
## 4. Conclusion
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), ZIF-8, was integrated into the chitosan/poly(vinyl alcohol) polymer blend to improve the removal of malachite green, as a cationic dye. ZIF-8/Chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol membrane was then coated on PVDF support, comprising a nonwoven backing to improve the mechanical stability of the membrane. Appropriate dispersion of ZIF-8 nanoparticles into the CPZP membrane was demonstrated by SEM images and XRD results. AFM and contact angle measurements verified that the PVDF support surface becomes smoother by the introduction of the CPZ layer. The BET analysis showed a high surface area of the CPZP membrane without a noticeable hysteresis effect. Central composite design (CCD) was employed to maximize the flux of distilled water and the rejection of dye solution using the CPZP membrane. The optimal conditions were calculated as; PEG of $10\mathrm{\wt\%}$ , chitosan equals to $3\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and the membrane thickness of $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The experimentally-measured flux of 78.94 $(\mathrm{L}.\mathrm{m}^{-2}.\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ and rejection of 90.32 $(\%)$ were obtained at the optimal conditions, in good agreement with the model-predicted flux and rejection, respectively. The value of FRR (92.00) during the fouling test showed the appropriate antifouling property of the CPZP membrane. The CPZP membrane offered a comparable performance compared to similar chitosan-based membranes; however, the trans-membrane pressure was noticeably (1 bar) lower than those applied for similar membranes.
According to the outcomes of the current work, all the research hypotheses including, excellent mechanical stability, high permeability and appropriate dye rejection were successfully achieved.
